3.3 Procedural Generation Loops
Separate world generation into several deterministic loops.
Loop A: Macro generation
Runs when an unexplored route segment is first generated.
	1	Select the next biome using biome-transition weights.
	2	Roll a biome length between its minimum and maximum.
	3	Generate an approximate terrain and elevation profile.
	4	Construct a feasible railway alignment.
	5	Reserve mandatory service stops and special landmarks.
	6	Define regional resource and weather modifiers.
Loop B: Trackside generation
Runs independently for each 1 km chunk.
	1	Read the chunk seed.
	2	Generate eligible structure candidates.
	3	Test railway clearance and terrain suitability.
	4	Apply spacing, rarity, and biome rules.
	5	Assign an archetype and spatial footprint.
	6	Reserve navigation, interaction, and unloading areas.
	7	Generate resource nodes and environmental details.
	8	Save deterministic placement metadata.
Loop C: Structure generation
Runs when a structure's footprint is established.
	1	Select the structure archetype.
	2	Generate its floor and room topology.
	3	Apply the archetype's room compatibility rules.
	4	Place entrances, exits, hazards, containers, and landmarks.
	5	Check connectivity and traversal requirements.
	6	Assign loot and enemy-spawn points.
	7	Generate local navigation data.
	8	Validate that all required rooms are reachable.
	9	Cache the resulting layout for reuse.
Loop D: Runtime simulation
Runs for active structures and the train.
	•	Update moving machinery, lights, doors, power, and environmental hazards.
	•	Run hostile AI only when appropriate.
	•	Simulate train physics and critical subsystems.
	•	Reduce update frequency for distant or inactive systems.
	•	Persist meaningful state changes.
Deterministic generation rule
Use separate random-number streams for independent generation tasks.
WorldSeed
 ├── BiomeSeed(chunkID)
 ├── TracksideSeed(chunkID)
 ├── StructureSeed(structureID)
 ├── InteriorSeed(structureID)
 └── LootSeed(structureID, containerID)
If a player re-enters a previously visited structure, its layout and original loot assignment must not reroll merely because the structure unloads and reloads.
Important: Deterministic generation does not replace persistence. If a player empties a crate, that crate must remain empty after unloading and returning.
3.4 Railway Clearance and Placement Constraints
Every structure candidate must pass a spatial validation stage.
Required checks:
	•	Minimum distance from the track centerline.
	•	Clearance from train-car collision volumes.
	•	Safe approach and parking areas where required.
	•	Sufficient surrounding space for its intended footprint.
	•	Reachable entry points for the expected player locomotion abilities.
	•	Valid NavMesh generation or an equivalent navigation solution.
	•	No placement inside mandatory track geometry.
	•	No collision with another critical landmark.
	•	No blocked exit caused by terrain or another generated room.
A structure may be rejected, repositioned, or regenerated using a bounded number of retries. When all retries fail, skip it rather than breaking railway continuity.

4. Dynamic Procedural Structures
4.1 Structure Spawning Architecture
Structures should be placed through a layered selection system instead of choosing a random building independently at every position.
The generation system evaluates five factors:
	1	Biome compatibility: Does the structure belong in this environment?
	2	Travel distance: Has enough distance passed since the last structure of this type?
	3	World pacing: Has the player recently encountered another major landmark?
	4	Progression relevance: Is the structure's reward appropriate for the game's current progression?
	5	Spatial feasibility: Can the facility physically fit beside the railway?
