5.2 Graph-Based Layout Generation
Represent the interior as a graph.
	•	Nodes represent rooms.
	•	Edges represent doors, corridors, ladders, stairs, or other connections.
	•	Node attributes define room type and gameplay purpose.
	•	Edge attributes define access restrictions and traversal costs.
Generation algorithm
	1	Select a start room and one or more exits.
	2	Generate a target room count based on facility size.
	3	Build a connected graph with an appropriate number of branches.
	4	Assign mandatory room roles.
	5	Fill remaining nodes with compatible room modules.
	6	Apply the archetype's constraints.
	7	Place optional loops, shortcuts, and hidden rooms.
	8	Assign door states, hazards, and containers.
	9	Embed the graph into physical space.
	10	Validate paths from each entrance to at least one exit.
	11	Generate or update the NavMesh.
	12	Reject the layout if essential traversal conditions fail.
A graph that connects every room mathematically can still be physically impossible. Validate the actual generated geometry, not just the abstract topology.
