5.3 Constraints That Make Layouts Believable
Enforce logical spatial rules:
	•	A turbine hall needs sufficient ceiling height.
	•	A laboratory should not connect directly to an exposed cliff without an intended transition.
	•	An elevator shaft requires valid upper and lower connections.
	•	A reward room should not spawn without an accessible route.
	•	A locked room may require a key, lockpick, power restoration, or alternate entrance.
	•	A maintenance room should be close to equipment it services.
	•	Water hazards need consistent water volumes and valid navigation boundaries.
	•	Every mandatory exit must remain reachable using the player's intended capabilities.
	•	A room may have randomized props, but its physical collision layout must remain valid.
NavMesh integration
Generate a NavMesh or equivalent navigation representation after physical assembly and collision validation.
For large facilities, build navigation per room module and stitch compatible navigation boundaries when rooms connect. Rebuild only the affected area when a movable object changes traversal.
Mark surfaces according to traversal capability:
	•	Walkable.
	•	Slippery.
	•	Deep mud or snow.
	•	Water.
	•	Ladder-only.
	•	Drop-down only.
	•	Unreachable to ordinary enemies.
	•	Reserved for specialized enemy movement.
Players may be able to clamber over obstacles that the ordinary enemy NavMesh cannot traverse. Preserve intentional player advantage without allowing the enemy to become permanently stuck in an otherwise valid encounter.
5.4 Controlled Randomness
Pure randomness creates repetitive incoherence. Use authored variability instead.
Each facility should contain:
	•	1–2 recognizable defining features.
	•	2–4 optional routes or room variants.
	•	1–3 meaningful environmental hazards, depending on facility size.
	•	1 clear point of entry.
	•	At least one viable exit route.
	•	A loot distribution shaped by the facility's purpose.
	•	A low-probability special discovery.
For example, every hydro-dam should read as a hydro-dam from outside. The dam's turbine arrangement, maintenance routes, water state, and control-room layout can differ between instances.
