Locked Safe System
A locked safe should have a physical lock mechanism and an explicit interaction state.
Initial implementation:
	1	Player targets the safe and chooses to inspect or unlock it.
	2	The game checks whether the player has a compatible lockpick.
	3	A first-person close-up interaction begins.
	4	The player manipulates the lock through a short, skill-based minigame.
	5	Incorrect attempts generate audio and may increase the chance of attracting attention outside the safe zone.
	6	A successful opening changes the safe's persistent state to Unlocked.
	7	Loot is generated from the safe's assigned table and saved as a container inventory.
Avoid repeatedly rolling loot every time the safe is opened. Loot should be determined when the structure is generated or when the container is first activated, then persisted.
Industrial Crate System
Industrial crates are heavy, conspicuous, and associated with manufacturing areas.
	•	Opening takes longer than a small toolbox.
	•	Their large size supports multiple item stacks or a few bulky components.
	•	Selected crates may require a wrench, cutting tool, or powered opener.
	•	Some crates can be physically relocated with suitable equipment.
	•	Crates may be empty, damaged, or sealed, depending on their archetype.
	•	Their reward quality is controlled by facility type and security level.
