Each enemy owns a finite state machine. Perception, movement, animation, and combat should be separate components so new enemies can reuse the same architecture.
Enemy A: Shamblers
Identity: Slow, human-like figures with distorted movement, damaged clothing, and an unsettling lack of urgency.
Primary role: Basic threat that punishes careless movement and prolonged scavenging.
	•	Perception: Primarily sight and moderate sound detection.
	•	Movement: Slow NavMesh pursuit with occasional stumbling animations.
	•	Attack: Short-range melee strike.
	•	Weakness: Poor acceleration and limited ability to traverse certain obstacles.
	•	Counterplay: Keep moving, use cover, return to the train, or interrupt them with a suitable defensive tool.
	•	Spawn preference: Open yards, roads, forest edges, and industrial lots.
Their combat should be readable. A wind-up animation must signal an imminent hit.
Enemy B: Stalkers
Identity: Thin, fast-moving entities that remain at the edge of visibility and target isolated light sources.
Primary role: Pressure players who rely on handheld illumination without a backup plan.
	•	Perception: Sight, light emission, and line of sight.
	•	Movement: Fast when crossing open areas; cautious when approaching a target.
	•	Attack: Brief lunge, followed by a retreat or repositioning maneuver.
	•	Special ability: Disrupts a flashlight through a dedicated attack.
	•	Counterplay: Use alternate illumination, coordinate with teammates, break line of sight, and avoid pursuing it blindly.
Flashlight disruption must be implemented fairly.
On a successful special attack:
	1	The flashlight emits a visible or audible malfunction cue.
	2	The light flickers.
	3	The flashlight enters a disabled or degraded state.
	4	The player can reactivate it after a short recovery or complete a simple physical reset.
	5	The Stalker repositions rather than continuously locking the player out of all illumination.
Do not permanently destroy the player's main flashlight through an unavoidable hit. A breakable battery or replaceable component can be a separate, explicitly communicated equipment mechanic.
Enemy C: Blind Crawlers
Identity: Low, pale, many-limbed organisms that navigate dark spaces through sound, vibration, and proximity.
Primary role: Force deliberate movement through enclosed interiors and debris fields.
	•	Perception: Sound and local vibration rather than conventional sight.
	•	Movement: Climbing and floor-level locomotion across compatible surfaces.
	•	Attack: Fast lunge or short-range grab.
	•	Weakness: Loses reliable tracking when targets remain quiet and create separation.
	•	Counterplay: Crouch-walk, stop interacting with noisy machinery, throw a sound-producing object elsewhere, and avoid unnecessary sprinting.
Crawlers should not receive perfect information about a player's position at all times. Every sound stimulus should have a location and decay over time.
Enemy D: Echoes
Identity: Distant humanoid silhouettes that reproduce sounds from the surrounding environment, including fragments of the train's mechanical rhythm.
Primary role: Psychological pressure and misdirection.
	•	Perception: Oriented around investigating isolated players and recent sound events.
	•	Movement: Slow approach, intermittent stopping, and concealed repositioning.
	•	Attack: Primarily used as a lead-in to another threat or as a rare standalone encounter.
	•	Special behavior: Produces a sound from a misleading direction before attempting to move closer.
	•	Counterplay: Check visual landmarks, stay near teammates, and use deliberate movement rather than reacting to every sound.
Echoes should be uncommon. If every sound is deceptive, spatial audio loses its usefulness.
