Deep snow should produce compressed footfalls and displaced powder. Mud should produce suction sounds, visible disturbance, and heavier footsteps. Slippery surfaces should affect acceleration and turning, but ordinary walking must remain controllable.
Footstep sounds should be driven by the actual surface and movement speed, not simply by the currently equipped biome.
9.4 Clambering Onto Moving Train Roofs
Roof traversal is a high-priority tactile mechanic because it allows players to move between modules even while the train is in motion.
Detection
Define climbable surfaces through dedicated components:
ClimbableSurface
    SurfaceID
    EntryPoints[]
    ExitPoints[]
    SurfaceNormal
    MaximumApproachSpeed
    AllowWhileMoving
    RequiredClearance
    FallRiskClass
The player must approach a valid entry point, maintain an acceptable body orientation, and satisfy the surface's locomotion requirements.
Roof-clamber sequence
	1	The player approaches a roof edge or side ladder.
	2	A contextual prompt appears.
	3	The player initiates a clamber.
	4	A hand or foot contact sequence aligns the character to the climbable surface.
	5	The character transitions to a roof-traversal locomotion mode.
	6	The controller follows the train's moving reference frame.
	7	The player can walk, crouch, inspect, or prepare to cross a gap.
The climb animation should align to the actual train's movement rather than teleporting the player to a fixed world-space point.
Moving reference frame
When traversing a moving train, character velocity is a combination of train velocity and player motion relative to the train:
\[ \mathbf{v}_{\text{world}} = \mathbf{v}_{\text{train}} + \mathbf{v}_{\text{relative}} \]
For a stable player standing on the roof, relative motion is approximately zero. When the player runs forward along the roof, relative velocity is added to train velocity.
Use the same principle for jumping between train cars so the player retains appropriate forward momentum rather than freezing in world space when leaving a moving surface.
9.5 Jumping Between Shifting Cars
Train cars can move relative to one another due to track geometry, coupling motion, braking, and acceleration.
The jump system should account for:
	•	Current player position and velocity.
	•	Train speed and acceleration.
	•	Relative motion between the two cars.
	•	Gap width and vertical offset.
	•	Roof obstruction and clearance.
	•	Landing surface validity.
	•	Whether the destination is moving away or toward the player.
Landing procedure
	1	Detect an intended landing region on the destination car.
	2	Estimate relative position and velocity.
	3	Initiate the jump only when the target lies within a sensible reachability envelope.
	4	Apply normal airborne physics in the train-relative frame.
	5	On contact, validate that the player can stand on the target roof.
	6	Transition to a stable roof-traversal state.
If the player misses, use a consistent fall outcome. Depending on the geometry, the player might land on a side platform, grab an edge, collide with a safety rail, or fall to the ground. Do not force every miss into the same instant-death animation.
Safety and challenge
	•	Running across roofs should be possible but should not be required for routine locomotive operation.
	•	Handrails and designated paths provide safe routes.
	•	Damaged or detached cars may introduce genuine gaps.
	•	Weather may increase the chance of slipping, but slipping should remain predictable.
	•	Night 5 attacks can make exposed roof travel dangerous, forcing defenders to choose between safe interior routes and valuable firing positions.

