12.2 Roles and Shared Responsibilities
Roles should be recommendations supported by equipment, progression, and player specialization. They should not become fixed classes that prevent one player from helping with another job.
Role A: The Engineer
Primary responsibility: Keep the locomotive operational and improve its long-term performance.
Core activities:
	•	Load fuel and maintain the furnace.
	•	Monitor pressure, speed, heat, and mechanical warnings.
	•	Operate the throttle, brake, and other locomotive controls.
	•	Repair critical mechanical components.
	•	Diagnose engine faults.
	•	Manage power distribution to defensive systems.
	•	Lead emergency repairs during The Pursuit.
Recommended specialization benefits:
	•	Faster maintenance interactions.
	•	Better visibility into mechanical fault causes.
	•	Reduced material waste on routine repairs.
	•	Access to diagnostic information for advanced engine systems.
Trade-off: The Engineer is most effective when focused on the train, but must not be required to remain in the locomotive at all times.
Role B: The Scavenger
Primary responsibility: Bring the team the materials and equipment needed to expand and survive.
Core activities:
	•	Search structures and industrial containers.
	•	Identify valuable items.
	•	Carry and organize salvage.
	•	Locate alternate entrances.
	•	Unlock compatible safes.
	•	Collect fuel and basic materials.
	•	Identify rare structure variants and high-value containers.
Recommended specialization benefits:
	•	Better carry-weight efficiency.
	•	Faster container searches.
	•	Improved identification of certain hidden resources.
	•	More efficient lockpicking.
	•	Expanded field inventory options.
Trade-off: Specialized carrying equipment should still consume inventory capacity or weight budget. Avoid giving the Scavenger so much capacity that other players become irrelevant.
Role C: The Defender
Primary responsibility: Protect the train and support teammates during dangerous expeditions.
Core activities:
	•	Operate mounted weapons.
	•	Maintain ammunition supplies.
	•	Install defensive modules and armor.
	•	Watch for Night 5 attack telegraphs.
	•	Protect exposed teammates.
	•	Establish safe routes through hostile facilities.
	•	Respond to attacks on train cars.
Recommended specialization benefits:
	•	Faster mounted-weapon reloads.
	•	Improved weapon heat management.
	•	More efficient defensive equipment maintenance.
	•	Better information about exposed train modules and incoming attack direction.
Trade-off: Heavy weapons and armor consume construction space, materials, and ammunition. A heavily defended train should require meaningful compromises elsewhere.
Role D: The Medic/Chef
Primary responsibility: Maintain team recovery, food supplies, and expedition readiness.
Core activities:
	•	Cook ingredients into meals.
	•	Prepare emergency rations.
	•	Treat injuries and stabilize incapacitated players.
	•	Organize medical supplies.
	•	Maintain food preparation equipment.
	•	Monitor recovery resources.
	•	Distribute food and medical supplies before difficult encounters.
Recommended specialization benefits:
	•	More efficient medical-item use.
	•	Improved cooking yield.
	•	Better preparation of recovery-oriented food.
	•	Faster stabilization of incapacitated teammates.
	•	More reliable identification of medical resource requirements.
Trade-off: Food and medicine consume storage space and preparation time. Their primary value is improved resilience, not making players invulnerable.
