12.4 Shared Inventory and Workstation Access
The team should have multiple inventory layers:
	1	Player inventory: Items carried by an individual.
	2	Train storage: Shared resources accessible from designated storage points.
	3	Container inventory: Loot remaining in world structures.
	4	Machine inventory: Fuel, ammunition, crafting inputs, and other equipment currently loaded into specific systems.
	5	Protected progression inventory: Unique blueprint unlocks and other persistent milestones.
These inventories must remain logically separate, even when they are presented through one interface.
When an item moves between inventories, perform a single authoritative transaction. Do not remove it from the source only on the client or create it at the destination before the server validates the transfer.
12.5 Incapacitation and Recovery
Players should be able to become incapacitated rather than immediately losing all progress through death.
Suggested rules:
	•	Incapacitated players can be revived using an appropriate interaction.
	•	Medical supplies affect recovery speed or the amount of recoverable health.
	•	A player may be dragged or assisted in a limited set of conditions if implemented.
	•	Ordinary-night enemy pressure decreases when the player returns to the train perimeter, but downed players outside it still need rescuing.
	•	During The Pursuit, exposed players remain in danger even on the train.
	•	Death, abandonment, and recovery should have consistent rules for inventory ownership.
Recommended default: leave the player's backpack in the world after death, with a persistent recovery marker while the session remains active. A party should generally be able to recover resources by returning to that location rather than losing everything automatically.
For disconnects, preserve the reconnect record independently from ordinary death behavior, as specified in the save system below.

13. Persistent Saving and Disconnect Handling
13.1 The Two-Level Save Architecture
Trackbound needs two distinct kinds of save data:
A. Durable station checkpoint: The authoritative, persistent world save committed at a Biome Station.
B. Session recovery journal: A recoverable snapshot of the active run while travelling between stations.
The journal makes disconnects, crashes, and host migration manageable without weakening the intended role of stations as major progression checkpoints.
A station checkpoint is the official durable progression boundary. The session journal restores a compatible active session; it should not automatically grant every benefit of activating a station.
13.2 What the Biome Station Saves
When the team activates a Biome Station and commits a checkpoint, save a consistent world snapshot containing:
