Players should not need to memorize which of these categories are saved. The station activation interface should state clearly that the checkpoint includes train condition, shared storage, and progression.
13.3 Station Checkpoint Commit Procedure
Avoid writing dozens of independent files and declaring success before the entire state has been committed.
Use an atomic checkpoint protocol.
	1	Validate the station: Check that the server is authoritative and the station can commit a checkpoint.
	2	Establish a consistent snapshot: Record train, player, inventory, world, and progression state from a consistent simulation point.
	3	Serialize: Convert the snapshot into a versioned save representation.
	4	Write temporary data: Store the new snapshot in temporary files or a transactional storage layer.
	5	Validate integrity: Check data completeness, identifiers, and checksums where supported.
	6	Commit: Mark the new snapshot as the latest valid checkpoint.
	7	Retain recovery data: Preserve the previous valid checkpoint until the commit is verified.
	8	Notify clients: Report checkpoint success only after the server confirms the commit.
A failed checkpoint should preserve the last valid save and communicate the failure to the players.
13.4 Mid-Track Autosaving
The session journal must track the changes needed to reconstruct the active run.
Examples include:
	•	Train position and speed.
	•	Engine fuel and damage.
	•	Inventory transfers.
	•	Container contents.
	•	Player positions and statuses.
	•	Structure doors and machines that changed.
	•	Active crafting jobs.
	•	Completed encounters and event state.
