Easiest initial implementation
Recommendation: Implement pause-and-recover first, with a clear warning that only the last valid session journal may be recoverable if the host machine fails. Implement host migration only after the network authority and serialization systems are robust.
If a dedicated server disconnects a player, the session should continue normally. If the dedicated server itself shuts down, restore from the last valid session journal when permitted.
Case C: The player chooses Leave Session
	•	Confirm or roll back incomplete inventory transactions.
	•	Save a reconnect record where supported.
	•	Remove the player's avatar after the grace period.
	•	Do not trigger station activation.
	•	Do not duplicate dropped items or clone the character's inventory.
	•	Preserve the official station checkpoint independently from the session journal.
13.6 What Happens at a Biome Station?
A station is the formal place to commit persistent progress.
When the team activates it:
	1	The station presents the current train condition and save destination.
	2	Eligible players complete required station interactions.
	3	The server creates a consistent world snapshot.
	4	The server commits the checkpoint.
	5	The station records its identity, the time of activation, and the checkpoint version.
	6	The team receives explicit confirmation that the save succeeded.
	7	Earlier checkpoints remain available for recovery until their replacement is safely committed.
Stations can also support specialized gameplay services:
	•	Train reconstruction and car reconfiguration.
	•	Large-module installation.
	•	Fuel and material trading.
	•	Repairing heavily damaged equipment.
	•	Crafting advanced components.
	•	Reviewing route information and regional structure records.
A station should not automatically erase hostile encounters, regenerate previously emptied structures, or reset world progression. Those are separate world-state decisions.
