An event should contain enough context for other systems to respond without inspecting arbitrary hidden state.
For instance, OnPlayerLeftSafeZone should include the player identity, current world position, server time, and the relevant safe-zone identity. The Night Director can then evaluate threat eligibility without owning the player controller.
Avoid broadcasting every frame's movement through a general-purpose event bus. High-frequency simulation should use dedicated state replication and update loops.
14.3 AI Navigation and Performance
The world is infinite in extent but only a small fraction should be fully simulated at once.
Use three broad simulation levels:
Level
Example
Simulation detail
Near
Current structure, nearby players, active train modules
Full collision, interaction, AI and physics
Mid-range
Discovered structures, nearby track chunks
Reduced update frequency, limited ambient and structural simulation
Far
Unvisited terrain, distant scenery
Deterministic generation and simplified visual representation
Do not run full AI perception, collision, and pathfinding for enemies that cannot affect the current encounter.
For the train, keep core machine simulation active regardless of visual-detail distance. A vehicle should not consume a different amount of fuel simply because the camera is looking elsewhere.
Performance rules
	•	Pool frequently spawned particles and short-lived visual effects.
	•	Use simplified colliders for distant or non-interactive scenery.
	•	Build static room navigation before a player enters a facility.
	•	Avoid rebuilding an entire facility's NavMesh after opening a single door.
	•	Use spatial partitioning for interaction detection and threat proximity.
	•	Replicate gameplay changes, not every decorative animation.
	•	Rate-limit expensive generation and physics operations.
	•	Cache deterministic structure layouts.
	•	Set budgets for simultaneously active enemies, rigid bodies, and powered machines.
	•	Profile a complete moving train with four players, multiple active machines, and a generated facility—not isolated test scenes alone.
14.4 Client Prediction and Reconciliation
Player movement and immediate interaction feedback should feel responsive, even though the server determines authoritative outcomes.
For local movement:
	1	The client predicts movement using the current replicated world state.
	2	The server independently processes the movement request.
	3	The server sends the authoritative result.
	4	The client reconciles small differences without visibly snapping whenever possible.
For interaction:
	1	The client begins the expected animation.
	2	The server validates range, line of sight, permissions, item ownership, and target state.
	3	The server commits the outcome.
	4	The client updates inventory, animation state, and UI from the resulting authoritative data.
Use separate replication policies for high-frequency movement and low-frequency persistent state. A lever's physical animation may update continuously for nearby players, while a blueprint unlock only requires a confirmed state change.

15. Survival Balance and Failure Design
15.1 Failure Should Produce Consequences, Not Arbitrary Resets
Trackbound is a survival game, but the primary loop depends on continued attachment to the train.
The main consequences should be:
	•	Lost time.
	•	Consumed fuel.
	•	Damaged equipment.
	•	Expended ammunition.
	•	Reduced medical supplies.
	•	A dangerous recovery expedition.
	•	Lost or damaged unprotected cargo.
	•	Temporary loss of access to a module.
Permanent loss should be reserved for clearly communicated mechanics. Do not destroy an entire cooperative world because one player fell off a roof unless that is an explicit, agreed-upon game mode.
