15.3 Preventing Degenerate Strategies
Design constraints should prevent players from avoiding every risk through one trivial strategy.
Safe-zone camping: Normal nights are peaceful near the train by design. Meaningful night exploration is optional, but certain useful resources and discoveries should encourage prepared expeditions rather than force them.
Permanent sprinting: Terrain resistance, stamina, movement noise, and equipment load discourage indefinite sprinting without arbitrary speed caps everywhere.
Loot farming: Persistent container state prevents repeated unloading and reloading from regenerating loot.
Extreme train mass: Heavier modules increase required power and affect acceleration, braking distance, and fuel consumption.
Unlimited repair spam: Repairs consume appropriate resources and obey integrity limits.
Weapon stacking: Mount locations, firing arcs, power demand, ammunition, car mass, and construction capacity constrain defensive layouts.
Disconnect exploitation: Inventory transactions, death handling, and reconnect records are server-authoritative and cannot duplicate a player's equipment.
Enemy manipulation: Pursuit AI should respect its own rules and attack constraints rather than being fooled indefinitely by visual-obstruction edge cases.

16. Implementation Roadmap
Build Trackbound as a sequence of coherent, testable gameplay milestones. The first milestone should prove that the train, first-person locomotion, world generation, and persistence work together before the project expands into a large content catalog.
Phase 1: Playable Train Prototype
Deliverables:
	•	First-person movement and camera.
	•	One functional locomotive.
	•	Basic track spline and continuous forward movement.
	•	Fuel chunks, furnace door, throttle, and braking.
	•	One modular train car.
	•	Basic inventory and resource pickup.
Acceptance criteria:
	•	Players can board and leave a stationary train.
	•	The train starts and moves only when operating conditions permit.
	•	Fuel is physically transferred from inventory to the firebox.
	•	The train stops under the intended braking model.
	•	Basic machine controls work without using instant, abstract menu actions.
Phase 2: Modular Train and Building
Deliverables:
	•	Train construction grid.
	•	Module definitions and attachment sockets.
	•	Car connection rules.
	•	Storage, workshop, living, and defensive module prototypes.
	•	Component integrity and repair tools.
	•	Basic crafting interface.
Acceptance criteria:
	•	Players can build and remove supported modules without leaving invalid gaps in the car configuration.
	•	Modules have correctly replicated inventories and operating states.
	•	Damage affects the appropriate module.
	•	Manual repair consumes resources and restores integrity.
Phase 3: Infinite Route and Biomes
Deliverables:
	•	Deterministic world seed.
	•	One-kilometer chunk generator.
	•	Railway continuity system.
	•	Biome transitions.
	•	Trackside candidate generation.
	•	Structure placement constraints.
	•	World streaming and coordinate management.
Acceptance criteria:
	•	The train can move continuously across multiple generated chunks.
	•	The same world seed recreates the same initial route and structure placement.
	•	Chunk transitions do not produce missing track, visible collision gaps, or abrupt coordinate errors.
	•	Terrain and structure generation remain within a predictable performance budget.
Phase 4: Structures, Interiors and Loot
Deliverables:
	•	At least four facility archetypes.
	•	Modular interior graph generator.
	•	Compatible room connectors.
	•	Navigation generation and validation.
	•	Containers, keys, lockpicks and loot tables.
	•	Persistent structure identities and loot states.
Acceptance criteria:
	•	Each archetype creates several valid layouts.
	•	Mandatory entries and exits remain reachable.
	•	Locked containers cannot be opened without satisfying their requirements.
	•	Loot remains unchanged when a structure unloads and reloads.
	•	Invalid layouts fall back safely without blocking the railway or trapping players.
Phase 5: Day/Night and The Forgotten
Deliverables:
	•	Authoritative world clock.
	•	Lighting, fog, and ambience transitions.
	•	Safe-zone boundary system.
	•	Enemy controller and perception framework.
	•	Shamblers, Stalkers, and Blind Crawlers.
	•	Pursuit encounter director and its initial entity prototype.
Acceptance criteria:
	•	Normal nights are peaceful for players on or within 15 m of the train.
	•	Hostile AI activates only when night and safe-zone eligibility rules are satisfied.
	•	Enemies disengage correctly when players return to the safe zone.
	•	Enemy behavior is readable and does not rely on unavoidable attacks.
	•	The fifth-night event overrides the ordinary rules deliberately and visibly.
Phase 6: Co-op Networking and Saving
Deliverables:
	•	Four-player server-authoritative session.
	•	Replicated train controls, interactions, loot and damage.
	•	Shared inventories and crafting.
	•	Player disconnect/reconnect records.
	•	Session recovery journal.
	•	Biome Station checkpoint transactions.
Acceptance criteria:
	•	All players see consistent train position, inventory, and module state.
	•	Two players cannot duplicate the same item through simultaneous pickup or crafting requests.
	•	A client disconnect does not corrupt shared inventories.
	•	Reconnection restores a validated player state.
	•	Station activation commits a consistent snapshot.
	•	An interrupted checkpoint never overwrites the last valid checkpoint with an incomplete save.
Phase 7: Pursuit, Progression and Content Expansion
Deliverables:
	•	Mounted heavy weapons.
	•	Multiple Pursuit attacks and phases.
	•	Weapon ammunition, heat and component damage.
	•	T3/T4 loot, blueprints, and advanced train modules.
	•	More biomes and structures.
	•	Advanced locomotion, environmental hazards, and quality-of-life systems.
Acceptance criteria:
	•	A four-player team can prepare, survive, and recover from The Pursuit.
	•	Different train configurations create viable, distinguishable strategies.
	•	Advanced upgrades improve performance without trivializing fuel and maintenance.
	•	Higher-tier loot provides meaningful choices rather than making lower tiers irrelevant.
17. Automated Testing and Quality Assurance
Procedural survival games require more than manual playtesting. Trackbound needs automated tests that validate generated content, simulation invariants, multiplayer transactions, and long-duration sessions.
