17.2 Multiplayer Race-Condition Tests
The server must preserve inventory and world-state integrity under simultaneous player actions.
Examples:
	•	Two players attempt to pick up the same coal chunk.
	•	Two players attempt to open the same safe.
	•	A player disconnects while transferring an item.
	•	An Engineer repairs a component while it receives damage.
	•	A player enters construction mode while another changes the same train configuration.
	•	The host disconnects while the station checkpoint is being committed.
	•	A client reconnects with an outdated inventory snapshot.
	•	Two players try to purchase or craft using the same shared resources.
Pass condition: Every operation commits once, fails explicitly, or rolls back safely. No transaction silently creates or destroys resources.
17.3 Simulation Invariant Tests
Implement assertions for rules that must never be violated.
Examples:
Invariant 1:
A player inside the normal-night safe zone
cannot receive damage from ordinary Forgotten enemies.

Invariant 2:
A container's committed loot cannot be generated twice.

Invariant 3:
A train module cannot occupy an invalid construction footprint.

Invariant 4:
A successful inventory transfer removes the item
from the source exactly once and adds it to the destination exactly once.

Invariant 5:
A successful checkpoint always references a validated,
complete save snapshot.

Invariant 6:
The Pursuit event has one authoritative state and cannot
start multiple independent copies on the same world.

Invariant 7:
A player disconnect cannot duplicate the player's inventory.
These assertions should be available in development builds and included in automated multiplayer tests.
