Canonical time configuration: Use a 48-minute cycle: 30 minutes of daylight (3 minutes dawn, 24 minutes full day, 3 minutes dusk) and 18 minutes of night. This replaces the earlier 15-minute night entry in the preliminary phase table. Divide the night into 3 minutes of nightfall, 9 minutes of deep night, 3 minutes of predawn tension, and 3 minutes of dawn transition.
Development Priority
The most important engineering dependency is the train simulation and its persistence model. Build those before expanding the world into dozens of structures or implementing elaborate hostile AI.
The recommended order is:
	1	Make the locomotive, fuel, braking, and first-person movement reliable.
	2	Establish the modular train and authoritative inventory transactions.
	3	Build deterministic world streaming and procedural structure validation.
	4	Add the safe zone and ordinary-night AI state machines.
	5	Implement station checkpoints and session recovery.
	6	Complete four-player co-op and Night 5.
	7	Expand biome variety, advanced loot, and high-fidelity interactions.
The goal is a game in which every system reinforces the same central experience: the railway makes the world feel endless, the train gives players somewhere to belong, and leaving that moving sanctuary creates a meaningful reason to prepare, cooperate, and return.